# A timer left un-dismissed on the alarm screen for a while - confirms the
# overtime counter actually counts UP between two checkpoints rather than
# freezing, going backward, or showing something broken. (Forcing
# TS_RUNNING into overtime directly via TestSetTimerRemaining was
# considered instead, but sweep_expiries() - called right after every
# TestSetTimerIndex mutation specifically to "catch an immediate overtime"
# - would immediately flag it and show the alarm screen anyway, so
# there's no reachable "quiet overtime row sitting in the list" state to
# test separately from this one.)
#
# Uses FIXED TestSetTimerRemainingDisplay values at both checkpoints, like
# every other migrated sequence - watching the counter tick doesn't
# require leaving it live: format_alarm_elapsed() reads
# effective_now_for()/TestSetTimerRemainingDisplay exactly like any other
# checkpoint, so each screenshot's value is still checked against real
# ground truth and pixel-reproducible run to run. What actually proves
# "ticks up correctly" here is the tolerance WINDOWS deliberately NOT
# overlapping - since both windows are individually verified against real
# ground truth AND checkpoint 2's window is strictly above checkpoint 1's,
# a passing run mathematically guarantees the real overtime increased
# between them, without needing either checkpoint's screenshot to show a
# live/unpinned value.
#
# The gap between the two SLEEPs below is 25s, not the original 5s - two
# rounds of retuning were needed:
# 1. An early attempt at a 5s gap (checkpoint values 4s/12s +/-3s)
#    occasionally failed, since this environment's real CLI-to-device
#    round-trip overhead turned out bigger than initial hand-measurement
#    suggested (measuring `pebble` calls directly in a standalone script
#    gave ~4-5s checkpoint-1 overtime; running the SAME steps through this
#    actual framework consistently measured 8s instead - always measure
#    through the real framework, not a hand-rolled approximation of it).
#    A 15s gap fixed that natively (checkpoint 1 ~8s, checkpoint 2 ~28s,
#    consistent across repeated native runs).
# 2. Running CONTAINERIZED (tests/functional/docker/), checkpoint 1 stayed
#    solid (5-8s across native and containerized, solo and under `-j4`
#    parallel load), but checkpoint 2 turned out much less stable across
#    environments/load than checkpoint 1: 28s (native), 22s (container,
#    solo), and something outside a +/-5s-tolerance window in a full-suite
#    `-j4` parallel container run - a 20+ minute full-suite run isn't
#    practical to iterate against directly, so this was retuned using
#    `tests/functional/docker/run_container.sh <this file>` for fast
#    single-sequence containerized measurement instead. The 25s gap (up
#    from 15s) and wider checkpoint-2 tolerance give real margin against
#    that cross-environment/load spread while keeping the two windows
#    ([-13,-3] and [-43,-27]) comfortably disjoint.
#
# NOTE: live-verified (2026-09-30) across native (tests/functional_
# framework/run_sequence.sh) and containerized (docker/run_container.sh)
# runs, not guessed; re-verify (checkpoint 2 especially) if this file's
# SLEEP durations change, ideally including at least one containerized
# check given checkpoint 2's demonstrated cross-environment spread.
IMPORT ../common/wipe_and_prep.seq

APPMSG --string 10000="$(printf 'Quick Timer\0375\0371')"
SLEEP 1
APPMSG --int 10020=0 10021=1 10022=5
SLEEP 1
SLEEP 7
APPMSG --int 10020=0 10029=-8 10030=5 10033=0 --string 10031="12:00"
SCREENSHOT alarm_just_fired_elapsed_near_0
SLEEP 25
APPMSG --int 10020=0 10029=-35 10030=8 10033=0 --string 10031="12:00"
# Renamed 2026-10-02 - "about_5s" was stale from before the gap above was
# retuned from an original 5s to the current 25s (see this file's own
# header comment on that history); the real value here is ~35s overtime.
SCREENSHOT alarm_elapsed_should_now_read_about_35s
TAP down
SLEEP 1
# Idle/stopped after Stop - tc_reset() puts it back to the full configured
# duration (5s), an exact, time-independent constant, safe to check
# tightly (paused/idle timers now respect the override too - see
# remaining_override_get() in main.c).
APPMSG --int 10020=0 10029=5 10030=2 10033=0 --string 10031="12:00"
SCREENSHOT dismissed
